Yes. And I agree with your instinct: the previous ObjectMapper + YAMLFactory + InputStream loader is more code than you need.
You can use Spring's configuration mechanism for a separate YAML file. The key point is that @ConfigurationProperties does not inherently require the properties to come from application.yml; you can explicitly import another configuration resource.
For your case, I'd use Spring @ConfigurationProperties + @ImportResource/config-data import, depending on your Spring Boot version. The cleanest modern approach is spring.config.import.
Recommended approach
Keep:
application.yml
migrations/
├── v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
└── v2-to-v3-schema-migration.yml
Then explicitly import the migration file when that migration is being run.
However, there is an important architectural consideration: you don't want both V1→V2 and V2→V3 loaded into the same @ConfigurationProperties bean with the same prefix, because then you lose the clean version separation.
For your reusable migration framework, I'd actually recommend a slightly simpler pattern:
# v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml

schema-migration:
  from-version: 1
  to-version: 2

  entities:
    customer:
      applicable-columns:
        - field_b
        - field_c
      mandatory-columns:
        - field_b

    address:
      applicable-columns:
        - city
        - postal_code
      mandatory-columns:
        - city

    account:
      applicable-columns:
        - account_type
      mandatory-columns:
        - account_type
Then Java:
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "schema-migration")
public class SchemaMigrationConfig {

    private int fromVersion;
    private int toVersion;
    private Map<String, EntityMigrationConfig> entities;

    // getters/setters
}
And:
@Configuration
@EnableConfigurationProperties(SchemaMigrationConfig.class)
public class MigrationConfig {

}
But how does Spring know about the separate YAML?
That's the important part.
Spring Boot's configuration system can load additional configuration files using:
spring:
  config:
    import: classpath:migrations/v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
But putting that in application.yml means you've made the migration file part of the normal application configuration.
I don't think that's ideal for your requirement.
You want:
application.yml
     │
     └── normal application configuration


v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
     │
     └── migration-specific configuration
So I'd have the migration deployment/startup mechanism explicitly select the migration configuration file, rather than permanently importing every migration definition.
For example, you can run with:
--spring.config.additional-location=classpath:/migrations/v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
Then Spring loads it through its normal configuration mechanism, and @ConfigurationProperties binds it.
So your Java code remains very simple:
@Service
public class SchemaMigrationService {

    private final SchemaMigrationConfig migrationConfig;

    public SchemaMigrationService(
            SchemaMigrationConfig migrationConfig) {
        this.migrationConfig = migrationConfig;
    }

    public void migrateMainData(...) {

        EntityMigrationConfig config =
                migrationConfig.getEntities().get(entity);

        // use config.getApplicableColumns()
        // use config.getMandatoryColumns()
    }
}
Why this fits your architecture
You then have:
                  Spring Configuration
                         │
             ┌───────────┴───────────┐
             │                       │
      application.yml       Migration YAML
             │                       │
      normal application       V1 → V2
       configuration           definition
                                     │
                                     ▼
                           @ConfigurationProperties
                                     │
                                     ▼
                           MigrationConfig Bean
                                     │
                                     ▼
                           Migration Service
And for V2 → V3:
v2-to-v3-schema-migration.yml
             │
             ▼
     same Java config model
             │
             ▼
     same Migration Service
One caveat
If the application is running normally with application.yml and you dynamically want to choose between:
v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
v2-to-v3-schema-migration.yml
at runtime based on fromVersion/toVersion, @ConfigurationProperties isn't the best fit. Spring configuration is generally loaded as application configuration rather than as an arbitrary file selected per service call.
For your use case, I'd choose between:
Option A — migration is a deployment-time operation:
Use Spring Config Data + @ConfigurationProperties. This is what I'd recommend for your current migration.
Option B — migration files are selected dynamically at runtime:
Use the small dedicated MigrationConfigLoader we discussed earlier.
Given that you're triggering this as an internal migration during deployment, Option A is actually a very good fit and keeps your Java implementation considerably simpler.

YesYes. This can be a fully independent Jira focused only on the migration configuration framework. It should not depend on the database migration, CSV processing, or migration execution Jiras.
Jira Summary
Implement Externalized Schema Migration Configuration
Description
Implement an externalized configuration mechanism for schema migrations so that migration-specific rules are maintained separately from application.yml and can be reused for future schema transitions such as V1→V2, V2→V3, etc.
The configuration should be loaded and validated before any migration execution takes place.
Scope
1. Create migration configuration
Create dedicated YAML files under:
src/main/resources/migrations/
Example:
v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
The configuration should contain:
from-version
to-version
entity-level configuration
applicable columns
mandatory columns
Example:
schema-migration:
  from-version: 1
  to-version: 2

  entities:
    customer:
      applicable-columns:
        - customer_type
        - field_b
        - field_c
        - optional_field
        - conditional_field

      mandatory-columns:
        - customer_type
        - field_b

    address:
      applicable-columns:
        - address_type
        - city
        - postal_code

      mandatory-columns:
        - address_type
        - city

    account:
      applicable-columns:
        - account_type
        - currency
        - status

      mandatory-columns:
        - account_type
        - currency
2. Implement configuration model
Create Spring configuration classes using @ConfigurationProperties to represent the migration configuration.
Example structure:
SchemaMigrationConfig
 ├── fromVersion
 ├── toVersion
 └── entities
      ├── customer
      ├── address
      └── account
Each entity configuration should contain:
EntityMigrationConfig
 ├── applicableColumns
 └── mandatoryColumns
3. Configure Spring to load the migration YAML
Configure the application to load the selected migration YAML as an additional Spring configuration source.
The migration YAML should remain separate from the normal application configuration.
Example:
application.yml
        +
v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
The implementation should allow the same mechanism to be used for future migration definitions.
4. Validate loaded configuration
Implement configuration validation before migration execution.
Validation should include:
Configured from-version matches the requested source version.
Configured to-version matches the requested target version.
Requested entity exists in the configuration.
applicable-columns is configured.
mandatory-columns is configured.
Mandatory columns are a subset of applicable columns.
No duplicate column definitions.
Migration definition is not empty or incomplete.
Example:
Requested: V1 → V2

Loaded config: V1 → V2
        ↓
      VALID
        ↓
Migration can proceed
If the wrong configuration is loaded:
Requested: V1 → V2

Loaded config: V2 → V3
        ↓
      INVALID
        ↓
Migration must not proceed
Out of Scope
This Jira should not implement:
CSV reading
CSV validation
Database backup
MAIN schema changes
HISTORY changes
MAIN data migration
Migration audit insertion
Outbox event insertion
Reconciliation
Constraint application
Kafka publishing
Those will be handled by the migration implementation Jiras.
Suggested Classes
migration/
└── config/
    ├── SchemaMigrationConfig
    ├── EntityMigrationConfig
    └── MigrationConfig
Acceptance Criteria
[ ] Dedicated V1→V2 migration YAML is created under resources/migrations.
[ ] Configuration contains source and target schema versions.
[ ] Configuration supports multiple entities.
[ ] Entity configuration supports applicable and mandatory columns.
[ ] Configuration is loaded using Spring configuration mechanisms.
[ ] Configuration is not placed in application.yml/application.properties.
[ ] @ConfigurationProperties is used to bind the configuration.
[ ] Configuration validation is implemented.
[ ] Source/target version mismatch causes migration configuration validation to fail.
[ ] Invalid entity or column configuration causes validation to fail.
[ ] Configuration validation occurs before any database migration operation.
[ ] The implementation is reusable for future V2→V3, V3→V4, etc.
[ ] No CSV or database migration logic is included in this Jira.

Yes, I think a separate “Schema Migration Definition” implementation section is cleaner. Your main service flow can stay focused on runtime processing, while this section explains the reusable configuration behind it.
Schema Migration Definition
Purpose
Externalize schema-specific migration rules from the migration service.
Allow the same migration framework to be reused for future schema transitions such as V2→V3, V3→V4, etc.
Avoid hard-coding version-specific migration rules in Java code.
Definition contains
From Schema Version — source schema version.
To Schema Version — target schema version.
Entity configuration — entities covered by the migration.
Applicable Fields — fields that can be populated/updated as part of the migration.
Mandatory Fields — applicable fields that must have valid migration values.
Example:
schema-migration:
  from-version: 1
  to-version: 2

  entities:
    customer:
      applicable-columns:
        - field_b
        - field_c
        - customer_type
      mandatory-columns:
        - field_b
        - field_c

    address:
      applicable-columns:
        - address_type
        - city
        - postal_code
      mandatory-columns:
        - address_type
        - city
Loading & Usage
Migration definition is maintained as a version-specific YAML file.
Example: v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
Definition is loaded during application startup using Spring configuration.
The loaded definition is available to the Migration Service at runtime.
Migration request fromVersion / toVersion is validated against the loaded definition before migration starts.
Applicable fields from the definition drive:
Migration data validation
Fields updated in MAIN
Fields included in reconciliation
Reusability
v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
            ↓
      Migration Framework
            ↓
v2-to-v3-schema-migration.yml
            ↓
      Same Migration Framework
            ↓
v3-to-v4-schema-migration.yml
Key design point: the migration framework remains common, while the schema migration definition changes per version transition. This avoids creating separate migration implementations for every V1→V2, V2→V3, etc. transition.

Schema Migration Definition
Purpose
Externalize schema-specific migration rules.
Reuse the same migration framework for future versions (V2→V3, V3→V4, etc.).
Properties
From Version
To Version
Entity
Applicable Fields – fields that can be populated/updated during migration.
Mandatory Fields – applicable fields that must have valid migration values.
Loaded during application startup and available to the Migration Service.
Sample
schema-migration:
  from-version: 1
  to-version: 2

  entities:
    customer:
      applicable-columns:
        - field_b
        - field_c
        - customer_type
      mandatory-columns:
        - field_b
        - field_c
Reusability: Same structure/framework can be used for v2-to-v3-schema-migration.yml, v3-to-v4-schema-migration.yml, etc.

How the Schema Definition YAML Makes the Framework Reusable
The migration framework is generic, while the schema-specific migration rules are externalized in the YAML definition. This allows the same migration service and repository logic to be reused for future migrations such as V2→V3, V3→V4, without creating new migration-specific Java code or POJOs for each schema version.
Schema Definition Structure
schema-migration:
  from-version: 1
  to-version: 2

  entities:
    customer:
      applicable-columns:
        - field_b
        - field_c
        - customer_type
      mandatory-columns:
        - field_b
        - field_c
Key Properties and Significance
Property
Significance
From Version
Defines the source schema version for which the migration definition applies. Used to validate that the migration request and current schema are aligned.
To Version
Defines the target schema version to which the data is being migrated. Together with from-version, uniquely identifies the schema migration definition.
Entities
Defines the entities included in the migration. Each entity has its own schema-specific migration properties. This allows the same framework to support multiple entities without entity-specific migration code.
Applicable Columns
Defines the columns involved in the migration for the entity. These columns are used for CSV column validation, migration/update DML generation, and reconciliation. The migration framework references these definitions instead of hardcoding schema-specific column names in Java code.
Mandatory Columns
Defines the columns that must contain valid values after migration. These are used for mandatory-field validation and to determine which columns require the final NOT NULL constraints after successful migration.
How This Makes the Framework Reusable
The Java migration framework contains the common migration logic, while the YAML contains the schema-specific definition.
                Generic Migration Framework
                         │
                         │ reads
                         ▼
              Schema Migration YAML
                         │
          ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
          ▼              ▼              ▼
    Version Range     Entity      Column Definitions
          │              │              │
          │              │        ┌─────┴─────┐
          │              │        ▼           ▼
          │              │   Applicable   Mandatory
          │              │    Columns      Columns
          │              │
          ▼              ▼
       V1 → V2       Customer
For a future V2→V3 migration, the framework does not need a new migration implementation. A new definition can simply be provided:
v2-to-v3-schema-migration.yml
with the V2→V3 entity and column definitions.
The same framework then uses the YAML to determine:
Which entities are applicable
Which CSV columns are expected
Which columns are used in migration DML
Which columns are compared during reconciliation
Which columns are mandatory
Which columns require final NOT NULL constraints
Which schema-version transition is being performed
Key Design Principle
Keep migration logic generic in Java and keep schema-specific information in the YAML definition.
This avoids:
Hardcoded schema-specific column names in Java
Separate migration POJOs for every schema version
Separate migration services for V1→V2, V2→V3, etc.
Duplicating migration logic for every schema change
Instead, new schema migrations are primarily configuration-driven, with new Java logic required only when the migration introduces genuinely new transformation/business behavior that the generic framework cannot support.

